  "Number": 1,
  "Question": "What is the value of the following expression? \\[\\dfrac{2+4+6}{1+3+5} - \\dfrac{1+3+5}{2+4+6}\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-1 \\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{36} \\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{12} \\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{147}{60} \\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{43}{3} \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "First evaluate the two sums: \\(2+4+6=12\\) and \\(1+3+5=9\\). Both denominators are nonzero. Reduce the fractions, then use the common denominator \\(12\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{12}{9}-\\frac9{12}\n=\\frac43-\\frac34\n=\\frac{16}{12}-\\frac9{12}\n=\\boxed{\\frac7{12}}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice C.",
